      story: "In 1985 the psychologist Thomas Gilovich and colleagues analyzed basketball shooting records, found no evidence that hitting several shots in a row raised the chance of hitting the next, and concluded that the hot hand was an illusion. That became the accepted view for decades. Then in 2015 the economists Joshua Miller and Adam Sanjurjo showed the original analysis contained a subtle statistical bias, and once it was corrected, a hot-hand effect appeared to exist after all.",
